A||N||15||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||Giving the Little Duke "Bigger Muscles" ||||3||
Z||000000||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||01-06-2001||07:30 AM||||The 2.5L 4cylinder in my 1984 was rebuilt by the original owner recently.  It ran well, but there was no question it was a pushrod engine.<P>This week I replaced the fuel pump, starter, and alternator as they had failed, failed, and were wimpy respectively. While changing the alternator I did an oil change.  I used a Fram Duo-Gard filter with PTFE and some Castrol full synthetic 5W30 oil.  <P>What a difference!  The clatter of pushrods and lifters has been minimized.  The car sounds pretty serious as it pulls to 5,000 rpm.  Before all of this, there was very little power over 4,000 rpm.    <P>The next step is to replace the catalytic converter with a higher flow aftermarket version.  I could say that I don't expect much from the duke, but the truth is that when you have 92 Hp to start, it takes only 9 Hp to increase the power by 10%. I have seen many claims that the 2.5L Fiero engine is capable of 125 to 150 HP on several websites.  Here's an example:<BR> <A HREF="http://fiero2m4.homestead.com/Builtduke.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero2m4.homestead.com/Builtduke.html</A>   <P>What have you done to get more out of your 2.5L 4cyl engine?  What made the most difference?  Anybody got a Fiero with a real Super Duty engine?  Who has the most powerful Duke of the all?<p>[This message has been edited by LuckyTheyWereEverMade (edited 01-06-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000001||2M4 Dale||01-06-2001||07:47 AM||||I got some serious improvement after I installed a low temp thermostat, K&N air and oil filters, Fiero Store red wires and AC Delco RapidFire plugs. BIG improvement !!!||||reg||7
Z||000002||jerpro||01-06-2001||12:09 PM||||instead of advancing the motor manually you can but the msd adjustable timing control HUGE improvement. It is like giving your fiero a shot of nitrous. It kills gas mileage but hey! it's worth it.<BR>It is also very easy to install. 2 hours max (most of the time trying to find a good place to install it) I got the idea from fieroville.<BR>you can andjust with the timing control up to 15 extra degrees. Well worth it.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Only try your best, and if you don't succeed... TRY HARDER.||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||01-06-2001||01:42 PM||||jerpro, i read that quite a while back. i didn't fully understand it though. it sounded like a good idea, but i wasn't sure how good it would work or how safe it was.||||reg||1
Z||000004||jerpro||01-06-2001||01:53 PM||||safe it is. works also.<BR>Gas mileage is reduced and emissions can be aproblem (not here in mich though).<BR>msd is a reputable company and their products are top notch.<BR>If you have anymore questions just post them or e-mail me.<P>------------------<BR>Only try your best, and if you don't succeed... TRY HARDER.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fotofrank||01-06-2001||02:46 PM|||| I have a Holley TBI and an MSD ignition on my '84 SE. I use Champion plugs gaped to .065<BR>defeated the hot air to the air intake. I have a G-Force meter and my 92hp  Duke checks out at 104hp. I also have a v6 muffleron the car. I hope to install a Holley intake and Hooker tri-y header. If i have the engine rebuildt and Head ported I may have a nice goin' machine. <P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://fotofrank.homestead.com/foto1~ns4.html||||reg||10
